<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Titus Quinctius Flamininus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 229–174 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Flamininus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Titus Quinctius Flamininus was a Roman general and politician who defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> at Cynoscephalae in 197 BCE, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmian-games">Isthmian Games</a> of 196 BCE he proclaimed the freedom of the Greek cities from Macedonian rule, and Roman forces left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 194 BCE. He was elected consul in 198 BCE and censor in 189 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/titus-quinctius-flamininus.jpg" alt="Titus Quinctius Flamininus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael J. taylor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flamininus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Flamininus belonged to the minor patrician gens Quinctia, which had lost much political influence by the middle of the 4th century BCE. His grandfather Lucius Quinctius was flamen Dialis, the priest of Jupiter, and the cognomen Flamininus derives from that priesthood. The Quinctii were long allied to the Fabii, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> says that a Fabius Buteo was the nephew of Flamininus's wife. His career began unusually during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>, when several men skipped steps of the cursus honorum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 208 BCE Flamininus served as military tribune under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-claudius-marcellus-consul-166-bce">Marcus Claudius Marcellus</a>, who died in an ambush near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crotone">Crotone</a>. He was probably quaestor in 206 and was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a>, a Greek city that had defected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and held a Roman garrison. After his uncle died he took command there as propraetor before the age of 25, an achievement explained by the shortage of experienced commanders, and he became familiar with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> and culture. In 201 and 200 he sat on commissions settling the veterans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a>.</p><p>He stood for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> before he was 30. Two tribunes of the plebs vetoed his candidacy because he was too young and had held no curule office, but the Senate compelled them to withdraw. By lot he received the command of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a>, drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> out of most of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and defeated him at the Battle of the Aous. As proconsul he defeated Philip at Cynoscephalae in 197 BCE. Philip had to surrender the Greek cities he held and pay 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, but his kingdom was left as a buffer, which displeased the Achaean League. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmian-games">Isthmian Games</a> in Corinth in 196 BCE Flamininus proclaimed the freedom of the Greek states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 192 BCE Flamininus warned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> not to interfere with the Greek states, and he was present at the Battle of Thermopylae in 191 BCE. He was elected censor in 189 BCE, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-elder">Cato the Elder</a>. In 183 BCE he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prusias-i-of-bithynia">Prusias I of Bithynia</a> to seek the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, who killed himself to avoid being taken; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, many senators reproached Flamininus for causing the death of an enemy who had become harmless. He seems to have died around 174 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> hailed him as a liberator, minted coins with his portrait, and in some cities deified him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Titus Quinctius Flamininus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Against Rome the phalanx proved inflexible on broken ground: Philip V was defeated by <b>Titus Quinctius Flamininus</b> at the Battle of Cynoscephalae in Thessaly in 197 BCE, and Perseus' army was destroyed by Lucius Aemilius Paullus at the Battle of Pydna in 168 BCE, when Roman maniples exploited…</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Flamininus</b> proclaimed the freedom of the Greeks in 196 BCE the league was refounded under Roman patronage.</blockquote><a href="/state/euboean-league">Euboean League</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Flamininus</b> proclaimed Greek freedom in 196 BCE it became the seat of the revived Thessalian League and passed under Roman control in 146 BCE; it is a major Greek city today.</blockquote><a href="/state/larissa">Larissa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-194" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Titus Quinctius Flamininus"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-194">What was here in 194 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
